  "ca5323c5-a135-43f1-81b9-d6b0848d2949": {
   "id": "ca5323c5-a135-43f1-81b9-d6b0848d2949",
   "message": {
    "id": "ca5323c5-a135-43f1-81b9-d6b0848d2949",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1776042079.544,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "1. **Thucydides, *History of the Peloponnesian War*** (Strassler's *Landmark* edition). The original case study of rising vs. established power, alliance politics and domestic politics under war pressure. Read Books 1–2 and the Melian Dialogue if you're short on time.\n2. **John Lewis Gaddis, *On Grand Strategy*.** A readable synthesis across Augustus, Philip II, Lincoln, FDR; good on the gap between aspirations and capabilities.\n3. **Hal Brands, *What Good Is Grand Strategy?*.** Short and practical. How the idea actually worked (and failed) in US administrations.\n4. **Halford Mackinder, \"The Geographical Pivot of History\" (1904) + Nicholas Spykman, *America's Strategy in World Politics*.** The heartland vs. rimland debate that still shapes how people talk about Eurasia.\n5. **Rush Doshi, *The Long Game*.** An argument about China's strategy toward the US-led order, based on Chinese Communist Party documents. Read it alongside critics who think it overstates coherence.\n6. **Lawrence Freedman, *Strategy: A History*.** Long, but the best map of how \"strategy\" evolved from military to political to business usage.\n\nPair Mearsheimer with **Ikenberry, *After Victory*** if you want the liberal-institutionalist counterpoint to offensive realism."
     ]
    },
